  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I remove XMP metadata from a PDF using command line?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Use ExifTool: run 'exiftool -XMP:all= document.pdf' to remove only the XMP stream while leaving the Info dictionary intact. To remove all metadata from both stores simultaneously, run 'exiftool -all= document.pdf'. Verify the XMP stream was cleared by running 'exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf' — no output means the stream is gone. Always add -overwrite_original to skip the backup file, or omit it to keep a _original backup."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is XMP metadata in a PDF and why is it separate from normal metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "XMP (Extensible Metadata Platform) is an XML-based metadata standard introduced by Adobe in 2001 and now part of ISO 32000. Modern PDFs store metadata in two independent locations: the standard Info dictionary (what Acrobat's Properties panel shows) and the XMP stream (an embedded XML document). They are separate stores — clearing one does not clear the other. Most metadata removal tools only address the Info dictionary, leaving the XMP stream intact with all original fields including author name, creation timestamps, and software identifiers."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does exiftool -all= remove XMP metadata from PDFs?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. The ExifTool flag -all= removes all writable metadata from both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream in a single operation. It is the most complete free method for removing XMP metadata from a PDF. After running it, verify with 'exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf' — if no output is returned, the XMP stream was successfully cleared. Note: ExifTool strips metadata fields but does not perform a full file rewrite — incremental update history requires QPDF or Adobe Acrobat's Sanitize Document for complete removal."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why does XMP metadata remain after I remove PDF metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Most PDF metadata removal tools — including many online services and Acrobat's 'Remove Hidden Information' function — only clear the Info dictionary. The XMP stream is a separate embedded XML document that requires explicit targeting. If you run a metadata cleaner and then check with ExifTool's -XMP:all flag and still see fields, the tool you used did not remove the XMP stream. The solution is to use ExifTool with -all= or -XMP:all= which specifically targets the XMP stream."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I remove XMP metadata from a PDF on Mac?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "On Mac, install ExifTool via Homebrew: 'brew install exiftool'. Then run 'exiftool -all= -overwrite_original document.pdf' to remove all metadata including the XMP stream. Verify with 'exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf' — no output confirms success. macOS Preview does not remove XMP metadata — it adds its own Producer field whenever you save. The browser-based method at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover also removes XMP metadata without requiring installation."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the difference between removing XMP metadata and sanitizing a PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "XMP metadata removal strips the embedded XML metadata stream from the PDF. PDF sanitization is broader — it covers XMP removal plus embedded objects, hidden layers, incremental update history, comments, and annotations. ExifTool performs XMP removal but not full sanitization. Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function (Tools > Redact > Sanitize Document) performs a complete file rewrite addressing all of these layers. For most professional use cases, XMP removal with ExifTool is sufficient. For legal evidence, federal submissions, or high-security contexts, full sanitization is recommended."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I remove XMP metadata from a digitally signed PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Removing XMP metadata from a digitally signed PDF will invalidate the digital signature, because the signature cryptographically covers the file content including the XMP stream. After metadata removal, the signature will show as invalid in Acrobat. If the document needs both a valid signature and clean metadata, strip the metadata first, then apply the digital signature. Stripping metadata after signing defeats the purpose of the signature."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

