    <!-- DIRECT ANSWER - Featured snippet + AI extraction target -->
    <div class="direct-answer">
      <div class="da-label">Direct answer</div>
      <p>To remove XMP metadata from a PDF via command line: <code>exiftool -XMP:all= document.pdf</code> removes only the XMP stream. <code>exiftool -all= document.pdf</code> removes both the XMP stream and the Info dictionary simultaneously. Verify with <code>exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</code> - no output means the stream is gone.</p>
      <p>If XMP metadata persists after using another tool, that tool only cleared the Info dictionary. The XMP stream requires explicit targeting.</p>
    </div>

    <p>I've tested this myself: take a freshly exported Word-to-PDF document, run it through a popular online metadata remover, then check with ExifTool. The Author field vanishes from Acrobat's Properties panel. Clean, right? Then run <code>exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</code>. Your name is still there. The creation date is still there. The software version is still there. The online tool removed the Info dictionary and left the XMP stream completely untouched.</p>

    <p>This happens with most tools. It's not a bug - it's a scope limitation. Fixing it requires understanding why two metadata stores exist in the same file and how to target both.</p>

    <h2 id="what-xmp-is">#1. What XMP metadata actually is - and why it's separate</h2>

    <p>PDF files have carried metadata since version 1.0. The original mechanism - the <strong>Info dictionary</strong> - is a simple key-value block embedded near the PDF's cross-reference structure. Author, Creator, Producer, dates. That's what Acrobat's Properties panel reads. That's what most people mean when they say "PDF metadata."</p>

    <p>In 2001, Adobe introduced the <strong>Extensible Metadata Platform (XMP)</strong> - an XML-based metadata standard that gets embedded as a separate stream within the PDF file body. XMP carries the same fields as the Info dictionary, plus additional namespace-specific fields that the Info dictionary can't hold. It became part of the ISO 32000 PDF standard. Every PDF exported from a modern application - Word, Google Docs, InDesign, Canva - now carries both.</p>

    <div class="key-insight">
      <div class="ki-label">Why this matters</div>
      <p>The two stores are <strong>independent</strong>. Clearing the Info dictionary does not affect the XMP stream. Clearing the XMP stream does not affect the Info dictionary. A complete clean requires explicitly targeting both. Most tools, APIs, and scripts that process "PDF metadata" were written before XMP became ubiquitous - and never updated to handle it.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The XMP stream in a PDF is literally an embedded XML document. If you open a PDF in a text editor and search for <code>&lt;x:xmpmeta</code>, you'll find it. Everything between the opening and closing tags is your metadata, stored in plain readable XML, invisible in the document but accessible to anyone running ExifTool, pdfinfo, or any modern PDF parser.</p>

    <h2 id="what-survives">#2. What survives after a partial clean - real ExifTool output</h2>

    <p>This is the section that makes this guide different from every Stack Overflow answer on the topic. Here's what a "cleaned" PDF actually looks like when you inspect both stores.</p>

    <div class="before-after">
      <div class="ba-panel">
        <div class="ba-header before">
          <span class="ba-title">After standard online cleaner</span>
          <span class="ba-status">&#9679; Info dict only</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ba-body">
          <span class="c-comment"># Acrobat Properties shows:</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">Author    </span>: <span class="ba-val-clean">[empty]</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">Creator   </span>: <span class="ba-val-clean">[empty]</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">Producer  </span>: <span class="ba-val-clean">[empty]</span><br><br>
          <span class="c-comment"># But exiftool -XMP:all shows:</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">XMP:Creator</span>: <span class="ba-val-exposed">Jane Smith</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">XMP:CreateDate</span>: <span class="ba-val-exposed">2026:09:14</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">XMP:ModifyDate</span>: <span class="ba-val-exposed">2026:10:01</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">XMP:Tool</span>: <span class="ba-val-exposed">Microsoft Word</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ba-panel">
        <div class="ba-header after">
          <span class="ba-title">After exiftool -all=</span>
          <span class="ba-status">&#10003; Both stores</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ba-body">
          <span class="c-comment"># Acrobat Properties shows:</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">Author    </span>: <span class="ba-val-clean">[empty]</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">Creator   </span>: <span class="ba-val-clean">[empty]</span><br>
          <span class="ba-field">Producer  </span>: <span class="ba-val-clean">[empty]</span><br><br>
          <span class="c-comment"># And exiftool -XMP:all shows:</span><br>
          <span class="c-comment"># (no output)</span><br><br>
          <span class="ba-val-clean">XMP stream: cleared</span><br>
          <span class="ba-val-clean">Info dict: cleared</span><br>
          <span class="ba-val-clean">Both stores: verified</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>The left panel is a real pattern - not a cherry-picked edge case. It represents the majority of online "metadata removal" tools and many desktop applications that advertise metadata cleaning but only process the Info dictionary.</p>

    <h2 id="method-exiftool">#3. Method 1 - ExifTool command line (recommended)</h2>

    <p><a href="https://exiftool.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ExifTool</a> by Phil Harvey is the gold standard for metadata manipulation. It's free, open source, cross-platform, and used by digital forensics professionals worldwide. Critically, it is one of the few tools that explicitly targets both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream in a single operation.</p>

    <p>Install it first:</p>

    <div class="os-tabs">
      <div class="os-tab-buttons">
        <button class="os-tab-btn active" data-tab="mac">macOS</button>
        <button class="os-tab-btn" data-tab="linux">Linux</button>
        <button class="os-tab-btn" data-tab="windows">Windows</button>
      </div>

      <div class="os-tab-content active" id="tab-mac">
        <div class="code-block">
          <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
          <span class="code-label">macOS - install via Homebrew</span>
          <span class="c-comment"># Install Homebrew first if needed: brew.sh</span><br>
          <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">brew install exiftool</span><br><br>
          <span class="c-comment"># Verify installation</span><br>
          <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool -ver</span><br>
          <span class="c-ok">12.76</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="os-tab-content" id="tab-linux">
