    <h3>The core commands</h3>

    <div class="code-block">
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
      <span class="code-label">Remove XMP metadata - all scenarios</span>
      <span class="c-comment"># ── RECOMMENDED: Remove everything from both stores ──</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-flag">-all=</span> <span class="c-file">document.pdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># ── Remove ONLY the XMP stream (keep Info dict) ──</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-flag">-XMP:all=</span> <span class="c-file">document.pdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># ── Remove specific XMP fields (keep others) ──</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-flag">-XMP:Creator=</span> <span class="c-flag">-XMP:CreateDate=</span> <span class="c-flag">-XMP:ModifyDate=</span> <span class="c-file">document.pdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># ── Remove and overwrite original (skip the _original backup) ──</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-flag">-all=</span> <span class="c-flag">-overwrite_original</span> <span class="c-file">document.pdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># ── Batch: remove XMP from all PDFs in current folder ──</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-flag">-all=</span> <span class="c-flag">-overwrite_original</span> <span class="c-flag">-ext pdf</span> <span class="c-file">.</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># ── Batch: process folder recursively ──</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-flag">-all=</span> <span class="c-flag">-overwrite_original</span> <span class="c-flag">-r</span> <span class="c-flag">-ext pdf</span> <span class="c-file">./documents/</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout amber">
      <div class="callout-label">&#9888; The backup file behaviour</div>
      <p>By default, ExifTool creates a backup with <code>_original</code> appended (e.g. <code>document.pdf_original</code>) before modifying the file. This is intentional and useful. Add <code>-overwrite_original</code> to skip the backup and write directly. For batch operations on documents you're certain about, <code>-overwrite_original</code> keeps the folder clean. For individual sensitive documents, let the backup exist until you've verified the output.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Understanding the flags - what each one actually does</h3>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Flag</th>
            <th class="dark">What it removes</th>
            <th class="dark">XMP stream cleared?</th>
            <th>Info dict cleared?</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>-all=</code></td>
            <td>All writable metadata - both Info dictionary and XMP stream</td>
            <td><span class="check">&#10004; Yes</span></td>
            <td><span class="check">&#10004; Yes</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>-XMP:all=</code></td>
            <td>All XMP namespace fields - leaves Info dictionary untouched</td>
            <td><span class="check">&#10004; Yes</span></td>
            <td><span class="cross">&#10006; No</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>-PDF:all=</code></td>
            <td>PDF Info dictionary fields only - leaves XMP stream untouched</td>
            <td><span class="cross">&#10006; No</span></td>
            <td><span class="check">&#10004; Yes</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>-Author=</code></td>
            <td>Author from Info dictionary AND XMP:Creator simultaneously</td>
            <td><span class="partial-mark">~ Partial</span> (creator only)</td>
            <td><span class="check">&#10004; Yes</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>-XMP:Creator=</code></td>
            <td>Only the XMP dc:creator field - leaves all other XMP fields</td>
            <td><span class="partial-mark">~ Partial</span></td>
            <td><span class="cross">&#10006; No</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- MID-ARTICLE CTA -->
    <div class="article-cta">
      <div>
        <h3>No command line? Use the browser tool instead</h3>
        <p>Removes XMP stream and Info dictionary. Nothing uploaded. Works on any OS.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/pdf-metadata-remover" class="btn btn-light">Open PDF Metadata Remover &rarr;</a>
    </div>

    <h2 id="method-qpdf">#4. Method 2 - QPDF for structural removal</h2>

    <p>ExifTool removes metadata fields but does not rewrite the PDF file structure. Incremental updates - appended edit sessions - remain in the file body even after ExifTool runs. QPDF linearises and rewrites the complete PDF structure, which eliminates incremental update history alongside standard metadata removal.</p>

    <p>The combination of QPDF and ExifTool is the most thorough free approach for complete sanitisation:</p>

    <div class="code-block">
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
      <span class="code-label">QPDF + ExifTool - complete structural sanitisation</span>
      <span class="c-comment"># Install QPDF</span><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># Mac: brew install qpdf | Linux: sudo apt install qpdf | Windows: choco install qpdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># Step 1: Rewrite PDF structure (removes incremental updates)</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">qpdf</span> <span class="c-flag">--linearize</span> <span class="c-file">input.pdf</span> <span class="c-file">temp-rewritten.pdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># Step 2: Strip all metadata from the rewritten file</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-flag">-all=</span> <span class="c-flag">-overwrite_original</span> <span class="c-file">temp-rewritten.pdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># Step 3: Verify XMP stream is cleared</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-flag">-XMP:all</span> <span class="c-file">temp-rewritten.pdf</span><br>
      <span class="c-ok"># No output = XMP stream is gone</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># Step 4: Verify incremental updates are gone</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">grep -c</span> <span class="c-flag">"%%EOF"</span> <span class="c-file">temp-rewritten.pdf</span><br>
      <span class="c-ok"># Should return 1 - one %%EOF means no incremental updates</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># Rename to final output</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">mv</span> <span class="c-file">temp-rewritten.pdf</span> <span class="c-file">output-clean.pdf</span>
    </div>

    <h2 id="method-browser">#5. Method 3 - Browser tool (no installation)</h2>

    <p>For professionals who need XMP removal without command-line access - on a client machine, on a Chromebook, or in a workflow that doesn't permit software installation - the browser-based approach is the practical alternative.</p>

    <p><a href="/pdf-metadata-remover">FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover</a> uses pdf-lib to process files locally in the browser. The file never leaves your device. The tool applies three passes: setter methods on the Info dictionary fields, direct deletion from the trailer dictionary, and full XMP stream removal. The output file is available for download within seconds.</p>

    <p>For compliance or audit purposes: the browser processing means nothing is transmitted - you can verify this by opening DevTools → Network tab while the tool runs. Zero outbound requests carry your file content.</p>

