    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2 id="frequently-asked-questions" class="faq-section-title">#10. Frequently asked</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">How do I remove XMP metadata from a PDF using command line?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Use ExifTool. <code>exiftool -XMP:all= document.pdf</code> removes only the XMP stream. <code>exiftool -all= document.pdf</code> removes both the XMP stream and the Info dictionary. Verify with <code>exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</code> - no output confirms the XMP stream is gone. Add <code>-overwrite_original</code> to skip creating a backup file.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What is XMP metadata in a PDF and why is it separate from normal metadata?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">XMP (Extensible Metadata Platform) is an XML-based metadata standard introduced by Adobe in 2001, now part of ISO 32000. Modern PDFs store metadata in two independent locations: the Info dictionary (visible in Acrobat's Properties panel) and the XMP stream (an embedded XML document). They are completely separate - clearing one does not clear the other. Most metadata removal tools only target the Info dictionary, leaving the XMP stream intact.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Does exiftool -all= remove XMP metadata from PDFs?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Yes. The <code>-all=</code> flag removes all writable metadata from both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream in one operation. After running it, verify with <code>exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</code> - if no output is returned, the XMP stream was cleared. ExifTool removes metadata fields but does not perform a full file rewrite - use QPDF before ExifTool if you also need to eliminate incremental update history.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Why does XMP metadata remain after I remove PDF metadata?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Most PDF metadata removal tools - including many online services and Acrobat's "Remove Hidden Information" function - only clear the Info dictionary. The XMP stream is a separate embedded XML document requiring explicit targeting. If you clean a file and then check with <code>exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</code> and still see fields, the tool you used did not address the XMP stream. Use ExifTool with <code>-all=</code> or <code>-XMP:all=</code> to specifically target it.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">How do I remove XMP metadata from a PDF on Mac?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Install ExifTool via Homebrew: <code>brew install exiftool</code>. Then run <code>exiftool -all= -overwrite_original document.pdf</code>. Verify with <code>exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</code> - no output confirms success. macOS Preview does not remove XMP metadata - it adds its own Producer metadata when you save. The browser-based method at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover also removes XMP without any installation.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What is the difference between removing XMP metadata and sanitizing a PDF?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">XMP removal strips the embedded XML metadata stream. PDF sanitization is broader - it covers XMP removal plus embedded objects, hidden layers, incremental update history (edit sessions appended to the file), comments, and annotations. ExifTool performs XMP and Info dictionary removal but not full sanitization. Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function performs a complete file rewrite addressing all layers. For most professional use cases, ExifTool is sufficient. For legal evidence or federal submissions, full sanitization is recommended.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Can I remove XMP metadata from a digitally signed PDF?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Technically yes, but it invalidates the digital signature. The XMP stream is part of the signed content - removing it breaks the cryptographic hash that the signature covers. After removal, the signature shows as invalid in Acrobat. If you need both clean metadata and a valid signature, strip the XMP first, then apply the digital signature. The correct workflow is: create document → strip metadata → sign. Not: create → sign → strip.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="legal-note">Tool limitations: ExifTool removes metadata fields from both the Info dictionary and XMP stream but does not perform a complete file rewrite. Incremental update history, embedded object metadata, and forensic residue in file slack space require QPDF linearisation or Acrobat Sanitize Document for complete elimination. Verify all removal operations with ExifTool before sharing sensitive documents. No tool can guarantee complete removal across all PDF versions and configurations.</p>

